WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:09.440
The country just held the most important presidential
election in its history.

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:15.480
A field of 13 candidates was narrowed down
to two for the final vote.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:20.200
Fernando Haddad represented the leftist Workers’
Party, which has been in power for much of

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:22.050
the past two decades.

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:24.260
He garnered just under 45% of the vote.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:30.180
Far-right candidate Jair Bolsonaro, who won
55% of the vote, has been elected Brazil’s

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:34.030
next President, marking a
dramatic shift in the country’s politics.

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:39.179
That’s because Bolsonaro is a longtime congressman
who is known for his ultra conservative and

00:00:39.179 --> 00:00:40.499
deeply offensive rhetoric.

00:00:51.539 --> 00:00:54.350
His rhetoric has sparked widespread protests
and outrage.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:59.260
It became so bad that at a campaign
rally, the candidate was stabbed by a man

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:01.360
claiming he was “on a mission from God.”

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:07.200
But, despite the backlash, Bolsonaro drew huge crowds to his rallies and skyrocketed

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:08.900
to the top of the polls.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:13.280
So how did this relatively unknown congressman
garner so much support?

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:16.140
And what does that mean for Brazil’s democracy?

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:25.080
Brazil hasn’t been a democracy for long.

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:31.850
The country was ruled by a military regime
from 1964 to 1985. It was an era marked by violence,

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:36.230
torture, exile, and censorship.

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:41.810
It was during this military regime that Jair
Bolsonaro began his career as an army captain.

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:47.060
When the regime fell in 1985 and Brazil became
a democracy, Bolsonaro entered politics as

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:53.390
a staunch far-right conservative; eventually
becoming a congressmen for Rio de Janeiro.

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:58.909
He served seven terms in congress but remained
controversial because of his extremist remarks.

00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:06.159
And he continued to show an affinity for the
military dictatorship.

00:02:06.159 --> 00:02:08.800
Just look at the photos in his congressional
office.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:13.200
The portraits on the wall are presidents from
Brazil’s military regime.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:16.280
As a presidential candidate, Bolsonaro didn’t change.

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:19.200
He openly calls called for bringing back some
of the military regime’s tactics.

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:40.319
In fact, Bolsonaro has picked a retired army general
as his running mate who has openly suggested

00:02:40.319 --> 00:02:42.760
that a military coup could be a possibility.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:53.390
This time though, the pro-military message struck a chord.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:04.240
For many Brazilians, particularly the young
voters in Bolsonaro's base, the military regime’s

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:06.610
brutality has faded into history.

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:11.099
Instead, Bolsonaro’s praise of the armed
forces has resonated with his supporters at

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:14.040
a time when crime has been soaring in the
country.

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:20.819
In 2016, Brazil’s homicide rate was nearly
six times that of the US.

00:03:20.819 --> 00:03:24.310
In a country that shares borders with some
of the biggest cocaine producing countries

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:29.100
in the world, much of Brazil’s violence
stems from drug gang rivalries.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:33.280
Mismanagement of public security funds by
government officials has left police in the

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:37.490
country underpaid and underprepared to deal
with the mounting violence.

00:03:37.490 --> 00:03:41.660
Bolsonaro’s pro-military stance has made
him “a champion of law and order”.

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:46.180
For many, even though his rhetoric calls back
to a brutal dictatorship; Bolsonaro is seen

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:48.680
as a solution for a country in crisis.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:10.099
But Brazil’s crisis isn’t just about homicides.

00:04:10.099 --> 00:04:14.319
The country is still reeling from the biggest
corruption scandal in its history.

00:04:14.319 --> 00:04:17.049
And Bolsonaro has capitalized on that too.

00:04:18.049 --> 00:04:20.760
“For weeks, Brazil has been consumed by allegations

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:26.280
of corruption and bribery at the highest level
involving politicians and business leaders.”

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:30.920
A massive corruption investigation, known
as Operation Car Wash uncovered a money laundering

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:35.720
scheme that funneled billions into the pockets
of politicians -- including members of major

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:37.720
political parties.

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:41.949
Bolsonaro seized the opportunity to position
himself as a political outsider and promised

00:04:41.949 --> 00:04:43.639
to end corruption.

00:04:43.639 --> 00:04:48.740
Here he is leading protests against Dilma
Rousseff, Brazil's president during the scandal.

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:52.590
He lashed out at the Worker’s Party, that
Rousseff belonged to, and whose members had

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:54.520
been prosecuted for corruption.

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:09.880
Bolsonaro was actually one of the pivotal
votes for Rousseff’s impeachment in 2016,

00:05:09.889 --> 00:05:14.360
when he openly dedicated his vote to an army
colonel accused of widespread torture and

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:15.610
murders during Brazil’s dictatorship:

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:30.290
The corruption scandal also helped Bolsonaro
by removing his strongest opponent.

00:05:30.290 --> 00:05:34.430
“Convicted for corruption, the man better
known as “Lula” was forced to bow out

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:36.100
of October’s presidential race.”

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:40.919
In September [2018], ex-President Lula
da Silva who was in jail for corruption, was

00:05:40.919 --> 00:05:43.780
banned from running in the presidential race

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:48.580
Even from jail, Lula was polling far above
any other presidential candidate.

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:53.180
But when Lula was officially barred from the
race, Bolsonaro soared in the polls.

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:57.220
His final opponent in the presidential race,
Fernando Haddad, belongs to the same party

00:05:57.220 --> 00:06:00.460
as Lula, but he didn't drawn didn’t draw
the same level of support.

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:03.200
In part because of the increase in crime and

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:07.680
rampant corruption, the public’s faith in
the government has eroded over the years.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:12.100
Their trust in congress, the presidency, the supreme court are all at an all time

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:13.100
low.

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:17.389
Meanwhile, the public’s view of the armed
forces is relatively favorable — with nearly

00:06:17.389 --> 00:06:20.180
8 out of 10 Brazilians supporting the institution.

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:28.100
For Brazilians fed up with the political ruling
class, Jair Bolsonaro is offering an alternative.

00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:43.620
But for many others, Bolsonaro's offensive comments

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:48.620
and anti-democratic solutions are a reminder
of Brazil's authoritarian past.

